  later grows enough interactive surface to justify a framework, the store/scheduler modules port over unchanged — it's
  the rendering layer you'd rewrite, and that's the cheap part.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · What stack should this be built on? → Vanilla ES modules (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Last question before I lay out the design.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether reminders can escape the tab. A "task overdue" reminder you only see when you're already looking at
  the tasks page is close to worthless — the overdue styling on the task itself already tells you that. The Web
  Notifications API can put an OS-level popup on screen while the user is on another tab, which is where the value
  actually is.

  - [1mIn-page only[0m — a notification tray with an unread badge, plus toasts. No permission prompt, one code path, nothing
    to fall back from. But reminders only ever land where the user is already looking.
  - [1mIn-page plus opt-in OS notifications[0m — same queue, with a thin adapter that also calls [38;5;153mNotification[39m when the user
    has granted permission. Permission requested from an explicit click in settings, never on page load (browsers
    penalise that, and users reflexively deny it). Degrades cleanly: denied or unsupported means in-page only. Cost is
    one adapter module and a permission-state branch. Still can't reach a closed tab — that needs a service worker and,
    realistically, a server.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m the second. It's a small amount of extra code sitting behind the same interface, and it's what
  makes the time-based half of the feature earn its keep. The constraint to accept either way is that a closed tab gets
  nothing; if that turns out to matter, it's the signal that you actually need a backend.
